VIGOR TUTOR

This tutorial teaches vigor by asking you to edit this file. Start it with:

    vig tutor

Do the exercises rather than only reading them. Your changes are held in the
buffer until you explicitly write them. To discard every tutorial change and
reload the original file, use:

    :e!

Press Esc whenever you are unsure which mode you are in.


LESSON 1 — NORMAL MODE AND MOVEMENT

Vigor starts in Normal mode. Normal-mode keys move the cursor or perform
commands; they do not insert text.

                    k
                    ↑
              h  ←     →  l
                    ↓
                    j

Exercise:

  1. Move around this paragraph with h, j, k, and l.
  2. Hold j to move down through the tutorial.
  3. Try the arrow keys, Home, and End.
  4. Press 0 to move to column zero and ^ to move to the first non-blank.
  5. Press $ to move one position past the end of a line.

Vigor intentionally permits the cursor one position past the final character
in every mode. This differs from vi but makes cursor behavior consistent.

Useful larger movements:

    w / b       next / previous small word
    e           end of small word
    W / B / E   corresponding whitespace-delimited WORD movements
    gg / G      first / last logical line
    12G         logical line 12
    Ctrl-D/U    half-page down / up

A count repeats a motion. Try 3w, 2j, and 4e on this line:

    one two three four five six seven

Vigor calls newline-delimited lines logical lines. When wrapping is enabled,
one logical line may occupy several display rows.


LESSON 2 — LEAVING AND RELOADING

Commands beginning with : appear in the bottom message bar and execute when
you press Enter.

    :q          close the current buffer, refusing unsaved changes
    :q!         close it and discard unsaved changes
    :w          write changes
    :wq         write and close
    :qa         quit all buffers, refusing unsaved changes
    :qa!        quit all and discard unsaved changes

Exercise:

  1. Read this whole lesson first.
  2. Press Esc, type :q!, and press Enter.
  3. Restart with "vig tutor".
  4. Return here with /LESSON 2 followed by Enter.

You can instead use :e! to discard changes and reload this named buffer
without leaving vigor.


LESSON 3 — INSERTING TEXT

The following commands enter Insert mode:

    i       insert at the cursor
    a       insert after the cursor
    I       insert at the first non-blank
    A       insert at the end of the logical line
    o       open a logical line below
    O       open a logical line above

Press Esc to return to Normal mode. Unlike vi, vigor does not move the cursor
left when Insert mode ends.

Exercise 1: use i to make the first line match the second.

    This sentnce is missig letters.
    This sentence is missing letters.

Exercise 2: use A to finish this line, then press Esc.

    Append text to the end of this

Exercise 3: place the cursor on this line, press o, type a new logical line,
and press Esc. Undo it afterward with u.

Insert-mode editing:

    Enter       split the logical line; copy indent when autoindent is on
    Tab         insert spaces to the next four-column tab stop
    Backspace   delete backward or join with the previous logical line
    Delete      delete under the cursor
    arrows      move without leaving Insert mode

Bracketed terminal pastes are inserted literally and normalize line endings.


LESSON 4 — DELETING, REPLACING, AND CHANGING

Normal-mode character commands:

    x       delete under the cursor
    X       delete before the cursor
    rX      replace under the cursor with X
    s       delete under the cursor and enter Insert mode
    ~       toggle character case
    J       join with the next logical line

Exercise: use x to remove the extra letters.

    Thee quuick browwn foxx jumpedd.

Operators combine with motions:

    d{motion}       delete
    y{motion}       yank (copy)
    c{motion}       change and enter Insert mode

Examples:

    dw      delete to the next word
    de      delete through the end of the word
    d$      delete to the end of the logical line
    dd      delete a logical line
    yy      yank a logical line
    cc      change a logical line
    D       delete to end of line
    C       change to end of line
    Y       yank to end of line

Exercise: use dw repeatedly to remove the unrelated words.

    Delete blue the paper words bicycle that do not moon belong.

Counts apply to operators and motions. Both 2dw and d2w delete across two word
motions. Use 2dd to delete two logical lines and then press u to restore them:

    temporary line one
    temporary line two
    keep this line

When :set nodelcopy is active, ordinary deletes preserve the register. The
special yd{motion} operator deletes while copying even under that option.


LESSON 5 — UNDO, REDO, AND DOT REPEAT

    u           undo
    Ctrl-R      redo
    .           repeat the latest change

Vigor stores full-buffer undo snapshots. Insert sessions may be split into
periodic undo chunks after word boundaries.

