  1. Use x to correct the repeated letters below.
  2. Undo several corrections with u.
  3. Redo them with Ctrl-R.

    Fiix thhe repeaated leetters.

Dot repeat replays the latest change. Place the cursor on each "bad" and use
dw once, then . on the remaining occurrences:

    bad keep bad keep bad keep


LESSON 6 — YANKING AND PASTING

Deleted or yanked text enters one shared unnamed register.

    p       paste after the cursor, or below for linewise text
    P       paste before the cursor, or above for linewise text

Exercise: move the lines into alphabetical order using dd and p or P.

    delta
    bravo
    alpha
    charlie

Try yy followed by p to duplicate a logical line. Undo afterward.

Clipboard integration is controlled with:

    :set clipboard=auto
    :set clipboard=osc52
    :set clipboard=off


LESSON 7 — VISUAL SELECTIONS

    v       Visual character mode
    V       Visual Line mode

Move after entering Visual mode to extend the highlighted selection. Then use:

    d or x      delete selection
    y           yank selection
    c           change selection
    gc          toggle comments on selected logical lines

Exercise:

  1. Put the cursor at the start of "select this" below.
  2. Press v and move to the end of those words.
  3. Press y.
  4. Move to the blank line and press p.

    select this but not this

Visual Line mode selects whole logical lines. Press Esc to cancel either
Visual mode.


LESSON 8 — TEXT OBJECTS, INDENTATION, AND COMMENTS

Operators support useful text objects:

    diw / daw       inner / around small word
    diW / daW       inner / around WORD
    di( / da(       inside / around parentheses
    di[ / da[       inside / around brackets
    di{ / da{       inside / around braces
    di" / da"       inside / around double quotes
    di' / da'       inside / around single quotes

The same objects work with c and y. Try ci" inside this text:

    Change "the words inside these quotes" and leave the quotes.

Line commands:

    >>          indent by four spaces
    <<          remove up to four leading spaces
    gcc         toggle the configured line comment

Counts apply to all three. Set the comment prefix when needed:

    :set comment=#
    :set comment=//


LESSON 9 — FINDING TEXT

Character finds operate within one logical line:

    fx / Fx     find x forward / backward
    tx / Tx     stop before x forward / backward
    ; / ,       repeat / reverse the latest character find

Try fa and then ; on:

    alpha beta gamma delta

Search commands accept Python regular expressions:

    /pattern       search forward
    ?pattern       search backward
    n / N          repeat in the same / opposite direction
    * / #          search whole word under cursor forward / backward
    g* / g#        search partial word under cursor forward / backward

Exercise: type /platypus and press Enter, then use n and N.

    platypus one; platypus two; platypus three

Searches wrap around the buffer. Lowercase literal patterns ignore case; a
capital letter or any regular-expression metacharacter makes the search
case-sensitive. Lowercase word searches with *, #, g*, and g# also ignore
case. Invalid regular expressions produce a message instead of moving. Search history is available with Up and Down.

Enable or disable persistent match highlighting with:

    :set hlsearch
    :set nohlsearch

Place the cursor on a bracket and press % to move to its match:

    function(one, [two, {three: four}])


LESSON 10 — SUBSTITUTE

Substitute uses a Python regular expression and replacement:

    :s/old/new/         first match on the current logical line
    :s/old/new/g        every match on the current logical line
    :%s/old/new/g       every match in the buffer
    :2,5s/old/new/g     matches on logical lines 2 through 5

Any punctuation delimiter may replace /. For paths, this is often clearer:

    :s|old/path|new/path|g

Exercise: move to the line below and execute :s/thee/the/g.

    Usually thee best time to see thee flowers is spring.

Vigor does not implement Vim's substitute-confirmation flag.


LESSON 11 — FILES AND THE WORKING DIRECTORY

All explicit relative paths use one global working directory. It begins as
the directory from which vigor was invoked.

    :pwd            show the working directory
    :cd path        change it globally
    :cdb            change it to the focused file's directory

Path completion works for :cd, :e, :w, :read, and :rgf. Type part of a path
and press Tab. Multiple matches appear in a menu:

    Up / Down       change selection
    Tab / Shift-Tab next / previous selection with wrapping
    Enter           insert the selected path
    Esc             hide the menu

File commands:

    :e path         open another buffer
    :e!             reload the current named buffer
    :w [path]       write, optionally under a new name
    :read path      insert a file below the cursor
    :read !cmd      insert command output below the cursor

If a write target has missing parent directories, vigor asks before creating
them.


LESSON 12 — MULTIPLE BUFFERS

Each open file has its own cursor, viewport, and undo history.

    :new                new unnamed buffer
    :ls                 list buffers
    :next / :n / :bn    next buffer
    :prev / :p / :bp    previous buffer
    :k / :bdelete       delete a clean buffer
    :k! / :bdelete!     force deletion

Leader shortcuts begin with Space:

    Space n         next buffer
    Space N         previous buffer
    Space d         delete the current clean buffer
    Space w         toggle wrapping

The status bar shows [current/total] when more than one buffer is open.


LESSON 13 — WRAPPING, NUMBERS, AND SCROLLING

    :set wrap / nowrap
    :set wrapmove / nowrapmove
    :set number / nonumber
    :set relativenumber / norelativenumber
    :set scrolloff=N

With wrap enabled, long logical lines occupy multiple display rows. With
wrapmove, j, k, Up, and Down move through those display rows. With nowrapmove,
they continue to move by logical lines.

Space w toggles wrap without changing the configured wrapmove behavior.

Manual viewport commands:

    Ctrl-E / Ctrl-Y     scroll down / up without moving the cursor

In nowrap mode, vigor horizontally scrolls to keep the cursor visible.


LESSON 14 — SHELL COMMANDS AND FILTERS

    :!command           run a shell command and show compact output
    :read !command      insert command output below the cursor
    :%!command          replace the whole buffer with command output
    :2,5!command        filter logical lines 2 through 5
    :!!command          send the whole buffer and open output in a new buffer

Examples:

    :!pwd
    :read !printf 'inserted by a command\n'

Shell commands run in the same global working directory used by file paths.
Bang and read commands have a short timeout; builds use the separate quickfix
commands in the next lesson.


LESSON 15 — BUILDS AND QUICKFIX

A diagnostic producer emits navigable lines in this form:

    path:line:column: message

Run any producer into quickfix:

    :qf !command

Configure and run a build:

    :set makeprg=make
    :make
    :make clean

The optional producer wrapper handles common GCC/Clang and Python output:

    :set makeprg=scripts/vig-diagnostics make
    :qf !scripts/vig-diagnostics python3 program.py
    :qf !scripts/vig-diagnostics --cwd subproject make

Quickfix shortcuts:

    Space c         return to the remembered quickfix buffer
    Space o         open the location on the current quickfix row
    Space j         open the next valid quickfix location
    Space k         open the previous valid quickfix location

Next/previous navigation skips context rows and stops at the boundaries. A
successful open shows the acted-on diagnostic line in the message bar.
Quickfix remembers the directory in which its producer ran, so a later :cd
does not reinterpret relative locations.

Ripgrep can also populate quickfix:

    :rg pattern [path]
    :rgf [path]          optional interactive fzf picker


LESSON 16 — CONFIGURATION AND HELP

Vigor reads simple set-style configuration unless VIG_NO_CONFIG is set:

    ~/.vigrc
    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vig/config

Later files override earlier ones. Example configuration:

    set number
    set relativenumber
    set scrolloff=3
    set nowrap
    set autoindent
    set hlsearch
    set clipboard=auto
    set makeprg=make

VIG_CONFIG=/path reads only the named configuration file.

Use :help to open vigor's concise help buffer. It is an ordinary buffer in
the buffer list; use :q to close it when other buffers are open. The complete
command reference is also available in reference.md.


VIGOR-SPECIFIC DIFFERENCES FROM VI

Remember these deliberate differences:

  - Esc from Insert mode leaves the cursor exactly in place.
  - The cursor may sit one position past the end of a logical line.
  - There is one unnamed register, with no named registers or macros.
  - Runtime behavior is implemented in one Python file with no curses or
    third-party dependencies.
  - Only the commands described by :help and reference.md are supported.


SUMMARY

    h j k l                 move
    i a I A o O             enter Insert mode
    Esc                     return to Normal mode
    x / dd / d{motion}      delete
    y{motion} / p / P       yank and paste
    c{motion}               change
    u / Ctrl-R / .          undo, redo, repeat
    v / V                   Visual / Visual Line
    / ? n N                 search
    :w / :q / :wq           write and quit
    :e / :next / :prev      manage buffers
    :pwd / :cd / :cdb       manage the global working directory
    :make / :qf             collect build diagnostics
    :help                   concise built-in help

You now know the core of vigor. Use :e! if you want to restore this tutorial
and repeat any exercise.
